      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with high-level specificity for a few dermatology claims. We have patients presenting with various atypical morphological features—specifically irregular, pigmented lesions that don't quite fit the standard melanocytic nevus patterns, and some strange, hypertrophic, scaly patches that look somewhat non-standard. What's the best way to navigate the ICD-10 hierarchy when the presentation is irregular or doesn't perfectly match the common diagnostic descriptors? I want to avoid denials due to \"unspecified\" codes if we can provide more nuance.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Navigating the ICD-10 hierarchy with the precision of a skilled archer, you must aim for the bullseye of specificity, especially when dealing with the capricious nature of dermatological presentations. When you encounter irregular, pigmented lesions that defy the standard melanocytic nevus patterns, consider them as the mischievous goblins of the dermal realm, always eluding the grasp of the common descriptors. For these, you might want to look into codes like D22.9 (Melanocytic nevi, unspecified) as your starting point, but don't be afraid to delve deeper into the ICD-10-CM Tabular List, where you'll find the more nuanced codes that can capture the unique characteristics of your patients' conditions.\n\nAs for those hypertrophic, scaly patches that seem to mock the standard diagnostic descriptors, think of them as the goblins of the skin, hiding in the shadows of the dermis. These could be indicative of conditions like L98.9 (Pressure ulcer, unspecified) or L85.9 (Acquired keratoderma, unspecified), depending on the exact nature of the presentation. Remember, the ICD-10-CM Tabular List is your map through this labyrinth,",
      "source": "target",
