      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's focus on the clinical indicators that should guide your code selection for keratinization disorders with highly irregular morphology. When differentiating between L85.9 (Acquired keratoderma, unspecified) and other keratinization disorders, consider the following clinical features:\n\n1. Lesion Location: L85.9 is typically used when the keratoderma is generalized or not otherwise specified. If the lesions are localized to specific areas, such as the palms or soles, you may need to consider other codes like L40.4 (Lichen simplex chronicus) or L40.5 (Other chronic dermatitis).\n\n2. Lesion Morphology: L85.9 is used for keratoderma with a normal or slightly thickened epidermis. If the lesions are highly irregular, with significant thickening or hyperkeratosis, you may need to consider codes like L85.6 (Other keratotic disorders) or L85.8 (Other specified keratotic disorders).\n\n3. Associated Symptoms: L85.9 is used when there are no associated symptoms such as itching, pain, or bleeding. If the patient reports these symptoms, you may need to consider other codes such as L30",
      "source": "target",
